  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "The position $x(t)$ of a particle (in meters) at time $t$ seconds is given by \n\n$$\nx(t)={t}^{2}-10t-2,\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the time at which the particle is stationary.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we need to calculate the velocity function. We can do that by taking the derivative of the position function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-10t-2) \\\\  & =2t-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m/s}.$ So, we set $v=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2t-10 & =0 \\\\ 2t & =10 \\\\ t & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary at $t=5\\,\\text{s}.$",
